# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
augustine-4.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 2 ways: augustine-4.logic.r1 and augustine-4.logic.r1.3 have "cw_first ∧ cw_intellectual"; augustine-4.logic.r1.2 has "cw_intellectual"; they differ when only "the created wisdom is the intellectual nature, which by contemplating the light is light" is true, which the second allows
augustine-4.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps augustine-4.logic.r1 and augustine-4.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "the created wisdom is the intellectual nature, which by contemplating the light is light" is true and "a certain created wisdom was created before all things" is false. Is S1, "For although we find no time before it, for wisdom was created before all things; not that Wisdom which is altogether equal and coeternal unto Thee, our God, His Father, and by Whom all things were created, and in Whom, as the Beginning, Thou createdst heaven and earth; but that wisdom which is created, that is, the intellectual nature, which by contemplating the light, is light.", true then?
augustine-4.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: augustine-4.logic.r1 has nothing from it; augustine-4.logic.r1.2 and augustine-4.logic.r1.3 have "we_righteousness"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
augustine-4.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps augustine-4.logic.r1.2 and augustine-4.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "we also are called God's righteousness" is false. Is S4, "For we also are called Thy righteousness; for so saith a certain servant of Thine, That we might be made the righteousness of God in Him.", true then?
augustine-4.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: augustine-4.logic.r1 and augustine-4.logic.r1.3 have nothing from it; augustine-4.logic.r1.2 has "cw_first"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
augustine-4.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps augustine-4.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "a certain created wisdom was created before all things" is false. Is S5, "Therefore since a certain created wisdom was created before all things, the rational and intellectual mind of that chaste city of Thine, our mother which is above, and is free and eternal in the heavens (in what heavens, if not in those that praise Thee, the Heaven of heavens?", true then?
augustine-4.md:1: argue/readings: S6 is read 3 ways: augustine-4.logic.r1 has "cw_first → no_time_before" and "cw_created → beginning_creation" and "beginning_creation → creator_before" and "time_not_yet" and "time_not_yet → ¬beginning_time"; augustine-4.logic.r1.2 has "cw_first → no_time_before" and "time_not_yet" and "time_not_yet → ¬beginning_time" and "beginning_creation" and "cw_created ∧ beginning_creation → creator_before"; they differ when only "a certain created wisdom was created before all things", "we find no time before the created wisdom", "the Eternity of the Creator Himself is before the created wisdom" and "time itself was not yet" are true, which the first allows; augustine-4.logic.r1.3 has "cw_first → no_time_before" and "time_not_yet" and "time_not_yet → ¬beginning_time" and "cw_created → beginning_creation"
augustine-4.md:1: argue/answered: S6: answered no, which keeps augustine-4.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "a certain created wisdom was created before all things", "we find no time before the created wisdom", "the Eternity of the Creator Himself is before the created wisdom" and "time itself was not yet" are true and "this wisdom is created", "the created wisdom took from the Creator the beginning of its creation" and "the created wisdom took from the Creator a beginning of time" are false. Is S6, "Because this is also the Heaven of heavens for the Lord);--though we find no time before it (because that which hath been created before all things, precedeth also the creature of time), yet is the Eternity of the Creator Himself before it, from Whom, being created, it took the beginning, not indeed of time (for time itself was not yet), but of its creation.", true then?
augustine-4.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: augustine-4.logic.r1 and augustine-4.logic.r1.2 have nothing from it; augustine-4.logic.r1.3 has "cw_created → creator_before"; they differ when only "this wisdom is created" is true, which the first allows
augustine-4.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: augustine-4.logic.r1 and augustine-4.logic.r1.3 have "no_time_before ∧ creator_before ∧ beginning_creation ∧ ¬beginning_time"; augustine-4.logic.r1.2 has "no_time_before ∧ creator_before"; they differ when only "we find no time before the created wisdom", "the Eternity of the Creator Himself is before the created wisdom", "the created wisdom took from the Creator the beginning of its creation" and "the created wisdom took from the Creator a beginning of time" are true, which the second allows
augustine-4.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps augustine-4.logic.r1 and augustine-4.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "we find no time before the created wisdom", "the Eternity of the Creator Himself is before the created wisdom", "the created wisdom took from the Creator the beginning of its creation" and "the created wisdom took from the Creator a beginning of time" are true. Is the conclusion, "Because this is also the Heaven of heavens for the Lord);--though we find no time before it (because that which hath been created before all things, precedeth also the creature of time), yet is the Eternity of the Creator Himself before it, from Whom, being created, it took the beginning, not indeed of time (for time itself was not yet), but of its creation.", true then?
